WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.275 --> 00:00:04.475
Attention lets each token pull information from every other token.

2
00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:08.250
We'll build the formula piece by piece, then run a tiny example

3
00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:09.075
by hand.

4
00:00:10.075 --> 00:00:13.225
Every token is projected into three vectors.

5
00:00:13.175 --> 00:00:15.875
The query says what a token is looking for.

6
00:00:15.862 --> 00:00:18.850
The key is what other tokens match against.

7
00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:22.087
And the value is what actually gets passed along once a match

8
00:00:21.937 --> 00:00:22.900
is found.

9
00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:25.500
Read it inside out.

10
00:00:25.487 --> 00:00:29.188
Q K transpose takes the dot product of every query with every

11
00:00:29.038 --> 00:00:32.525
key, giving a grid of raw similarity scores.

12
00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:36.025
Then we divide by the square root of d k, the key

13
00:00:35.875 --> 00:00:36.900
dimension.

14
00:00:36.912 --> 00:00:39.675
Hold that thought; it's the main event later.

15
00:00:40.375 --> 00:00:44.387
Softmax turns each row of scores into weights that are positive and

16
00:00:44.237 --> 00:00:45.525
sum to one.

17
00:00:45.575 --> 00:00:50.350
Finally, multiplying by V blends the value vectors using those weights.

18
00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:53.975
Here's a toy setup with key dimension four.

19
00:00:53.975 --> 00:00:57.513
We have one query, and three tokens, each with a key and

20
00:00:57.363 --> 00:00:59.050
a two dimensional value.

21
00:00:59.725 --> 00:01:02.125
Key one points the same way as the query.

22
00:01:02.113 --> 00:01:04.150
Key two is orthogonal to it.

23
00:01:04.112 --> 00:01:06.225
Key three overlaps partly.

24
00:01:07.225 --> 00:01:09.000
First, the dot products.

25
00:01:08.987 --> 00:01:12.200
Key one matches on two positions, so it scores two.

26
00:01:12.237 --> 00:01:15.450
Key two scores zero, and key three scores one.

27
00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:19.175
The key dimension is four, so we divide by two.

28
00:01:19.212 --> 00:01:22.750
That gives scaled scores of one, zero, and one half.

29
00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:27.850
Softmax exponentiates each score and divides by the total.

30
00:01:27.862 --> 00:01:31.350
The weights come out at roughly half, a fifth, and a third.

31
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:35.625
The output is the weighted blend of the values: about point eight

32
00:01:35.475 --> 00:01:37.425
one and point four nine.

33
00:01:37.438 --> 00:01:40.550
Key one dominates, but the others still contribute.

34
00:01:41.525 --> 00:01:45.975
Assume the query and key components are independent, with mean zero and

35
00:01:45.825 --> 00:01:49.850
variance one, which is roughly what initialisation gives you.

36
00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:53.962
The dot product is a sum of d k such products, each

37
00:01:53.812 --> 00:01:55.350
with variance one.

38
00:01:55.375 --> 00:01:58.550
So the variance of the score is d k, and its spread

39
00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:00.950
grows like the square root of d k.

40
00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:05.150
Dividing by the square root of d k brings the variance back

41
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:08.000
to one, no matter how wide the model is.

42
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:13.600
Even in our tiny example, skipping the scale sharpens the weights noticeably.

43
00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:18.175
Now picture a real model with d k of five hundred twelve.

44
00:02:18.175 --> 00:02:21.925
Scores have a spread of about twenty-three, so gaps of twenty or

45
00:02:21.775 --> 00:02:23.125
more are normal.

46
00:02:23.162 --> 00:02:25.975
Softmax becomes effectively one-hot.

47
00:02:26.625 --> 00:02:28.225
That's the real problem.

48
00:02:28.175 --> 00:02:33.000
The softmax gradient is proportional to w times one minus w, which

49
00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:36.000
is near zero when every weight is zero or one.

50
00:02:36.012 --> 00:02:37.400
Training stalls.

51
00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:41.488
Scaling keeps scores in the range where softmax still has a useful

52
00:02:41.338 --> 00:02:42.075
slope.

53
00:02:43.075 --> 00:02:46.775
To recap: compute scores, scale them by the square root of the

54
00:02:46.625 --> 00:02:51.125
key dimension, softmax them into weights, and blend the values.

55
00:02:51.113 --> 00:02:54.900
The scale is cheap, and it's what keeps attention trainable as models

56
00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:55.725
get wider.
